## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to reason with three-valued semantics in policy rule evaluation and to design a generic propagator for real-world constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene three-valued logic semantics
- Implication evaluation under unknown values
- Constraint propagation mechanisms
- Fixpoint iteration algorithms
- Practical design patterns for policy engines

## Code Example
```javascript
// Kleene three-valued logic implementation
function not(x) { 
  if (x === 'T') return 'F'; 
  if (x === 'F') return 'T'; 
  return 'U'; 
}

function and(x, y) { 
  if (x === 'F' || y === 'F') return 'F'; 
  if (x === 'U' || y === 'U') return 'U'; 
  return 'T'; 
}

function or(x, y) { 
  if (x === 'T' || y === 'T') return 'T'; 
  if (x === 'U' || y === 'U') return 'U'; 
  return 'F'; 
}

function implies(x, y) { 
  if (x === 'F') return 'T'; 
  if (x === 'U') return y === 'T' ? 'T' : 'U'; 
  return y; 
}

// Constraint propagator
function propagate(constraints, variables) {
  let changed = true;
  while (changed) {
    changed = false;
    for (const constraint of constraints) {
      const result = evaluate(constraint, variables);
      if (result === 'F') return false;
      // Update variables based on constraint results
      // Implementation depends on constraint type
    }
  }
  return true;
}
```

## Algorithm Outline
1. Initialize variables with given values
2. Iteratively apply constraints using Kleene semantics
3. Track variable changes until fixpoint reached
4. Return false if any constraint evaluates to false
5. Return true with final variable assignment otherwise

